# =============================================================================
# Shell Command Spec Discovery Engine (Slice 1 of the spec-library
# initiative: a searchable, publishable library of Gherkin BDD specs for
# PatLang itself, its own libraries, and shell/utility commands on the
# machine's PATH -- built by a program that systematically discovers and
# generates the contracts, not ad hoc one-off work).
#
# WHAT THIS LIBRARY IS FOR (read this before treating a spec as a
# requirement): every Gherkin scenario this tool writes is a DESCRIPTIVE
# record of what a component actually, observably does -- never a
# PRESCRIPTIVE statement of what it's supposed to or intended to do.
# This is not conformance testing; there is no external standard a
# command is being checked against, and a "verified" status means only
# "this scenario's Then-clause was independently confirmed to match the
# component's real, observed behaviour" -- not "this command is
# correctly implemented" (a command could be verified AND buggy AND
# still get documented accurately, warts included). The purpose is to
# build a body of GROUND-TRUTH FACTS this project's own inference and
# goal-seeking systems (the inductive synthesis engine, GOAP planning,
# self_healing_engine.patlang) can safely reason and build on top of --
# knowing exactly what a component really does is what makes it safe to
# compose into a larger plan; knowing what it was merely intended to do
# is not. Keep this distinction explicit in every spec's own wording,
# not just in this comment.
#
# This slice covers shell commands only. A local LLM (Ollama) writes the
# human-readable Gherkin spec, using exactly the prompt shape already
# proven to work: "provide BDD style specs for the windows <cmd> command,
# only write the gherkin no preamble, put the gherkin in backtick code
# block". That text is pure documentation -- it is NEVER executed as-is.
#
# SAFETY (hard requirement, not a guideline):
#   - Only commands listed in the allowlist below are EVER actually
#     executed by this tool, and only via the exact, hand-written
#     invocation shape given for each -- never with LLM-suggested or
#     otherwise unsanitized arguments.
#   - Every verification probe builds its OWN fixture files inside
#     spec_scratch_dir(), a directory this tool creates and fully
#     controls, and only ever reads/searches within it. No allowlist
#     entry mutates, deletes, formats, or writes anything outside that
#     one scratch directory -- read-only, query-only commands only.
#   - A command NOT in the allowlist still gets its Gherkin spec
#     generated (pure text generation, no execution involved) but is
#     explicitly marked "unverified -- not executed" in its metadata
#     sidecar, never silently treated as tested.
#
# VACUOUSNESS GUARD (the other hard requirement): a spec is only ever
# marked "verified" after this tool's OWN positive/negative probe pair
# shows the real command's output genuinely DIFFERS between a case where
# the condition holds and a mutated case where it deliberately does not
# -- the same "a check that can't tell right from wrong proves nothing"
# lesson already learned the hard way in this project's self-healing
# engine (see friendly_cli/lib/self_healing_engine.patlang's own history:
# a "CONTRACT PASS" string that printed unconditionally, regardless of
# correctness). A probe where positive and negative behave identically is
# reported as "vacuous", not silently accepted as green.
# =============================================================================

make a function called spec_scratch_dir returns path
  return "friendly_cli/spec_scratch"
end

# Windows console tools (findstr, find, ...) treat a bare "/" in an
# argument as an OPTION PREFIX, not a path separator -- a forward-slash
# path like "friendly_cli/spec_scratch/dbg.txt" gets misparsed as a run
# of bogus switches, silently truncating down to just the last segment
# ("dbg.txt"), which then fails to open. PatLang's own file host
# functions (write_file/read_file) handle forward slashes fine, so
# fixtures are created/read with them as usual -- only the ARGUMENT text
# handed to a native console utility needs backslashes. Found via a
# direct probe: `findstr PATLANG_MARKER_PRESENT friendly_cli/spec_scratch/dbg.txt`
# failed with "Cannot open dbg.txt"; the same file opened fine once the
# path was backslash-separated.
make a function called to_windows_arg_path takes p returns windows_path
  let sb = sb_new()
  let i = 0
  while i < p.length do
    if substr(p, i, 1) == "/" then
      sb_push(sb, "\\")
    else
      sb_push(sb, substr(p, i, 1))
    end
    let i = i + 1
  end
  return sb_str(sb)
end

make a function called shell_spec_dir returns path
  return "spec_library/shell"
end

# --- LLM-generated Gherkin (documentation only -- never executed as-is) ---
#
# Uses self_hosting/lib/ollama.patlang's ollama_chat_once -- Ollama's
# non-streaming REST API (raw sockets, "stream":false) -- rather than
# shelling out to `ollama run` via exec_capture. Two real, independent
# advantages this matters for: (1) `ollama run`'s interactive CLI writes
# a continuous stream of ANSI spinner/cursor-control escape codes to
# stdout while the model loads, which exec_capture faithfully captures
# right alongside the real reply -- noise extract_gherkin_block then has
# to parse through (harmless once it finds the fence, but wasteful);
# "stream":false gets back one clean JSON response with no such noise at
# all. (2) A cold model load can take well over a minute with no partial
# output via the CLI path either way -- the API path doesn't make that
# faster, but it does mean there's nothing to strip once the real reply
# does arrive.
# Fallback text-generation source when the primary (ollama, local, fast,
# free) engine's reply is unusable -- no fence found at all, or the
# extracted text is too short to plausibly be a real spec. Uses hermes
# (an already-installed AI-agent CLI with real web/tool-calling
# capability, `hermes -z PROMPT -m MODEL`), pinned to a FREE model
# tier rather than the paid default, per the user's own listed options:
#   poolside/laguna-xs-2.1:free, tencent/hy3:free,
#   poolside/laguna-s-2.1:free, inclusionai/ling-3.0-flash:free,
#   stepfun/step-3.7-flash:free
# `poolside/laguna-xs-2.1:free` is the default here (confirmed working
# via a direct manual probe, ~25s for a real Gherkin spec, and notably
# MORE accurate than the local model on findstr's real flag set). This
# is a genuine fallback tier, not a loop: called at most once per
# command, only when the primary source's output already looks
# unusable, mirroring the same "one bounded, hand-reviewed fallback,
# never open-ended retrying" discipline as verify_find's self-heal.
make a function called llm_generate_gherkin_via_hermes takes command_name, model_name returns result
  let prompt = "provide BDD style specs for the windows " + command_name + " command, only write the gherkin no preamble, put the gherkin in backtick code block"
  print("[SPEC DISCOVERY] Falling back to hermes (" + model_name + ") for '" + command_name + "'...")
  let reply = exec_capture("hermes", "-z", prompt, "-m", model_name)
  return extract_gherkin_block(reply)
end

make a function called llm_generate_gherkin_for_command takes command_name, model_name returns gherkin_text
  # A one-shot example of the exact expected shape, not just an
  # instruction to follow it -- small local models in particular follow
  # a worked example far more reliably than a prose rule alone. The
  # goal here is narrower than "always use backticks": what actually
  # matters is getting real Gherkin content back, not chit-chat/
  # preamble/refusal fluff -- the fence is a strong hint the model
  # usually follows when shown one, not a hard requirement enforced on
  # the receiving end (see the "looks_like_gherkin" relaxation below).
  let system_prompt = "You write BDD-style Gherkin specifications for command-line tools. Respond with ONLY the Gherkin, no preamble or explanation, wrapped in a ```gherkin code block. Example, for a hypothetical 'greet' command:\n```gherkin\nFeature: Greet Command\n\n  Scenario: Print a greeting\n    Given the user runs greet\n    When the command executes\n    Then it should print \"Hello\"\n```"
  let user_prompt = "Provide BDD style specs for the windows " + command_name + " command."
  print("[SPEC DISCOVERY] Querying LLM for '" + command_name + "' Gherkin spec via " + model_name + " (non-streaming API)...")
  let reply = ollama_chat_once(model_name, system_prompt, user_prompt)
  if str_starts_with(reply, "ERROR:") then
    print("[SPEC DISCOVERY] LLM query failed: " + reply)
    return [reply, false]
  end
  return extract_gherkin_block(reply)
end

# Content-based usability check -- deliberately NOT "was there a fence".
# LLM replies are non-deterministic across calls: the same prompt to
# the same model can come back properly fenced one time and not the
# next, even though the actual Gherkin content is equally good either
# way (confirmed via a direct repro -- a fresh call for a command that
# had escalated to hermes in an earlier run came back perfectly fenced
# on retry, same prompt, same model). Requiring the fence specifically
# was rejecting perfectly good content and escalating needlessly. What
# actually matters is "is this real Gherkin, not chit-chat/preamble/
# refusal fluff" -- checked here by looking for the two structural
# markers every real scenario has, regardless of whether backticks
# happened to survive.
make a function called looks_like_gherkin takes text returns yes
  return contains_text(text, "Feature:") and contains_text(text, "Scenario")
end

# Extracts the text between a ```gherkin fence and the next ``` fence.
# Falls back to the raw trimmed reply if no fence is found at all, so a
# differently-formatted reply is still captured (flagged via the
# "fenced" return value) rather than silently discarded -- fenced=false
# no longer means "discard this", see looks_like_gherkin above for the
# check that actually decides usability.
make a function called extract_gherkin_block takes raw_reply returns result
  let fence = "```gherkin"
  let start_idx = -1
  let i = 0
  while (i <= raw_reply.length - fence.length) and (start_idx == -1) do
    if substr(raw_reply, i, fence.length) == fence then
      let start_idx = i + fence.length
    end
    let i = i + 1
  end
  if start_idx == -1 then
    return [str_trim(raw_reply), false]
  end
  let end_idx = -1
  let j = start_idx
  while (j <= raw_reply.length - 3) and (end_idx == -1) do
    if substr(raw_reply, j, 3) == "```" then
      let end_idx = j
    end
    let j = j + 1
  end
  if end_idx == -1 then
    return [str_trim(substr(raw_reply, start_idx, raw_reply.length - start_idx)), true]
  end
  return [str_trim(substr(raw_reply, start_idx, end_idx - start_idx)), true]
end

make a function called verify_diff returns result
  # diff is a GNU/MSYS tool (unlike findstr/find/where, native Windows
  # console tools that misparse "/" as an option prefix) -- forward
  # slashes work fine as path arguments here, no to_windows_arg_path
  # needed. Positive: two genuinely IDENTICAL files -> real diff
  # behaviour is empty output. Negative: two files differing by one
  # line -> output must contain that line's actual new content, not
  # just "some output" (which could be a usage error or crash message
  # just as easily as a real diff).
  let dir = spec_scratch_dir()
  let file_a = dir + "/diff_a.txt"
  let file_same = dir + "/diff_same.txt"
  let file_diff = dir + "/diff_diff.txt"
  write_file(file_a, "line one\nline two\nline three\n")
  write_file(file_same, "line one\nline two\nline three\n")
  write_file(file_diff, "line one\nPATLANG_CHANGED_LINE\nline three\n")

  let same_out = exec_capture("diff", file_a, file_same)
  let same_ok = str_trim(same_out) == ""

  let diff_out = exec_capture("diff", file_a, file_diff)
  let diff_ok = contains_text(diff_out, "PATLANG_CHANGED_LINE")

  remove_file(file_a)
  remove_file(file_same)
  remove_file(file_diff)

  if same_ok and diff_ok then
    return ["verified", "identical files produced empty output; files differing by one line produced output containing that line's real new content -- genuinely distinguishes same from different, not just 'produced some output'"]
  end
  if (not same_ok) and (not diff_ok) then
    return ["vacuous", "identical files did NOT produce empty output, and different files did NOT show the real changed content -- this check cannot distinguish same from different"]
  end
  return ["inconsistent", "same_ok=" + ("" + same_ok) + " diff_ok=" + ("" + diff_ok) + " -- diff's real behavior did not match the contract this probe assumed"]
end

# --- Safety allowlist: ONLY these commands are ever actually executed ---
#
# Each verify_* function below is a hand-written, self-contained
# verification probe: it builds its own fixture file inside
# spec_scratch_dir(), runs the real command in a fixed, read-only
# invocation shape (never LLM-supplied arguments), and confirms a
# POSITIVE case (the searched-for text really is in the fixture) and a
# MUTATED NEGATIVE case (searching for text that genuinely is NOT)
# produce different, correctly-shaped output -- specifically, that the
# positive run's raw output contains the real matched line VERBATIM and
# the negative run's does not. A weaker check (e.g. "the search term
# itself doesn't appear in the output") would be vacuous -- neither
# findstr nor find echoes the search term back, so that check would
# trivially "pass" even if the command were never actually invoked at
# all. Returns ["verified", note] / ["vacuous", note] / ["inconsistent", note].

make a function called verify_findstr returns result
  let dir = spec_scratch_dir()
  let fixture = dir + "/findstr_fixture.txt"
  let marker_line = "PATLANG_MARKER_PRESENT here"
  write_file(fixture, "alpha line\n" + marker_line + "\nbravo line\n")
  let fixture_arg = to_windows_arg_path(fixture)

  let positive_out = exec_capture("findstr", "PATLANG_MARKER_PRESENT", fixture_arg)
  let positive_ok = contains_text(positive_out, marker_line)

  let negative_out = exec_capture("findstr", "PATLANG_MARKER_ABSENT_XYZ", fixture_arg)
  let negative_ok = not contains_text(negative_out, marker_line)

  remove_file(fixture)

  if positive_ok and negative_ok then
    return ["verified", "positive probe's raw output contained the real matched line verbatim; negative probe's did not -- the check genuinely distinguishes match from no-match"]
  end
  if (not positive_ok) and (not negative_ok) then
    return ["vacuous", "neither probe's output contained the matched line -- this check cannot distinguish a real match from no match, so it proves nothing and must not be trusted as a passing spec"]
  end
  return ["inconsistent", "positive_ok=" + ("" + positive_ok) + " negative_ok=" + ("" + negative_ok) + " -- findstr's real behavior did not match the simple substring-search contract this probe assumed"]
end

make a function called verify_which returns result
  # Positive: resolve a command genuinely on PATH (findstr, confirmed
  # present earlier). Negative: a deliberately-bogus name.
  #
  # REAL BUG FOUND HERE: this originally checked for "findstr.exe" in
  # the positive output, assuming a Windows-style path -- but this
  # `which` is Git's own MSYS build, whose POSIX-style output
  # (`/c/WINDOWS/system32/findstr`, confirmed via a direct manual probe)
  # has NO extension at all. Checking for "/findstr" (the resolved path
  # fragment) instead, and for Git which's own specific "not found"
  # error prefix ("no <name> in (") on the negative side -- checking
  # merely "the bogus name doesn't appear in the output" would be
  # vacuous, since that error message literally echoes the name it
  # failed to find as part of its own text.
  let positive_out = exec_capture("which", "findstr")
  let positive_ok = contains_text(positive_out, "/findstr")

  let negative_out = exec_capture("which", "patlang_definitely_absent_cmd_xyz")
  let negative_ok = contains_text(negative_out, "no patlang_definitely_absent_cmd_xyz in (")

  if positive_ok and negative_ok then
    return ["verified", "positive probe resolved a real path fragment (/findstr); negative probe's output matched which's own specific not-found error prefix -- genuinely distinguishes found from not-found"]
  end
  if (not positive_ok) and (not negative_ok) then
    return ["vacuous", "neither probe's output had the expected shape -- this check cannot distinguish found from not-found"]
  end
  return ["inconsistent", "positive_ok=" + ("" + positive_ok) + " negative_ok=" + ("" + negative_ok) + " -- which's real behavior did not match the contract this probe assumed"]
end

make a function called verify_where returns result
  # Same shape as verify_which, against native Windows where.exe, whose
  # own "not found" message ("INFO: Could not find files for the given
  # pattern(s).") is confirmed (via a direct manual probe) to contain no
  # ".exe" either.
  let positive_out = exec_capture("where", "findstr")
  let positive_ok = contains_text(positive_out, "findstr.exe")

  let negative_out = exec_capture("where", "patlang_definitely_absent_cmd_xyz")
  let negative_ok = not contains_text(negative_out, ".exe")

  if positive_ok and negative_ok then
    return ["verified", "positive probe resolved a real path ending in findstr.exe; negative probe's output contained no .exe at all"]
  end
  if (not positive_ok) and (not negative_ok) then
    return ["vacuous", "neither probe's output had the expected shape -- this check cannot distinguish found from not-found"]
  end
  return ["inconsistent", "positive_ok=" + ("" + positive_ok) + " negative_ok=" + ("" + negative_ok) + " -- where's real behavior did not match the contract this probe assumed"]
end

# --- Self-healing, bounded to exactly ONE fallback attempt, never a loop ---
#
# find.exe requires its search-string argument to literally CONTAIN a
# surrounding pair of double-quote characters as part of find's OWN
# parsing (confirmed via `find /?` and a direct manual probe -- `find
# PATLANG_MARKER_PRESENT file` fails with "Parameter format not
# correct"). exec_capture's underlying std::process::Command::args,
# though, builds each Windows argument following the platform's own
# quoting rules and ESCAPES any embedded `"` rather than passing it
# through raw -- so find.exe never receives the bare quote it requires,
# no matter how the PatLang-side argument string is written. That's a
# real, environment-specific limitation of the direct-exec_capture
# invocation shape, not a PatLang bug and not something retrying the
# SAME invocation differently can fix.
#
# The self-heal here is a SECOND, DIFFERENT, hand-authored strategy --
# not a retry of the first, and not LLM-improvised: write a tiny,
# throwaway .bat file (a real file this tool creates, controls, and
# deletes afterward) containing the find.exe invocation as STATIC TEXT,
# with a fully-qualified path to the real system find.exe (side effect
# of the same investigation: bypasses a second, unrelated problem --
# some PATH orderings on this machine resolve a bare "find"/"cmd" to a
# different tool of the same name entirely, e.g. Git's own GNU find,
# well before Windows' System32\find.exe is ever reached), then
# exec_capture's the .bat file itself with ZERO arguments -- Rust's own
# argument-escaping never touches the quote characters at all, because
# they're baked into the script file's own bytes, not passed as a
# runtime argument. Verified working via a direct manual probe before
# writing this into the tool.
#
# BOUNDED ON PURPOSE: this fallback is tried EXACTLY ONCE, only for
# find specifically (not a generic "try harder" loop any command could
# trigger), and if it also fails the result is reported honestly as
# still unverified -- there is no third strategy, no recursive retry,
# and no attempt to have an LLM invent a new invocation shape on the
# fly. A self-healing mechanism that keeps trying novel strategies
# against a fundamentally broken assumption is exactly the infinite-loop
# risk this design avoids by construction: one known root cause, one
# specific, hand-reviewed, pre-written countermeasure, tried once.
make a function called verify_find_via_batch_fallback takes fixture returns result
  let bat_path = spec_scratch_dir() + "/find_probe.bat"
  let marker_line = "PATLANG_MARKER_PRESENT here"
  let fixture_arg = to_windows_arg_path(fixture)

  let positive_bat = "@echo off\r\n\"C:\\Windows\\System32\\find.exe\" \"PATLANG_MARKER_PRESENT\" \"" + fixture_arg + "\"\r\n"
  write_file(bat_path, positive_bat)
  let positive_out = exec_capture(bat_path)
  let positive_ok = contains_text(positive_out, marker_line)

  let negative_bat = "@echo off\r\n\"C:\\Windows\\System32\\find.exe\" \"PATLANG_MARKER_ABSENT_XYZ\" \"" + fixture_arg + "\"\r\n"
  write_file(bat_path, negative_bat)
  let negative_out = exec_capture(bat_path)
  let negative_ok = not contains_text(negative_out, marker_line)

  remove_file(bat_path)

  if positive_ok and negative_ok then
    return ["verified", "direct exec_capture invocation failed due to a known argument-quoting limitation (find.exe needs a literal embedded quote character that Rust's own argument escaping never passes through raw) -- self-healed via a single, pre-written, fully-qualified batch-file fallback (not a retry loop, not LLM-improvised); the batch file's own static text supplied the quoting directly, and the positive/negative probe still genuinely distinguishes match from no-match"]
  end
  if (not positive_ok) and (not negative_ok) then
    return ["vacuous", "the batch-file fallback also could not distinguish a real match from no match -- self-healing attempted once and did not resolve the problem; needs manual review, not further automatic retries"]
  end
  return ["inconsistent", "positive_ok=" + ("" + positive_ok) + " negative_ok=" + ("" + negative_ok) + " -- the batch-file fallback ran but find's real behavior still did not match the simple substring-search contract assumed here"]
end

make a function called verify_find returns result
  let dir = spec_scratch_dir()
  let fixture = dir + "/find_fixture.txt"
  let marker_line = "PATLANG_MARKER_PRESENT here"
  write_file(fixture, "alpha line\n" + marker_line + "\nbravo line\n")
  let fixture_arg = to_windows_arg_path(fixture)

  let positive_out = exec_capture("find", "\"PATLANG_MARKER_PRESENT\"", fixture_arg)
  let positive_ok = contains_text(positive_out, marker_line)
  # REAL BUG FOUND HERE: this originally checked for the string
  # "Parameter format not correct", find.exe's error for a BARE,
  # unquoted search term -- but the actual failure this tool triggers
  # (confirmed via a direct debug probe of the real code path) is
  # different: "Access denied - \", produced once Rust's own argument
  # escaping has already mangled the intended `"..."` into malformed
  # text. Checking for the string this code path genuinely produces,
  # not the one from an earlier, differently-shaped manual test.
  let looks_like_quoting_failure = contains_text(positive_out, "Access denied")

  if positive_ok then
    let negative_out = exec_capture("find", "\"PATLANG_MARKER_ABSENT_XYZ\"", fixture_arg)
    let negative_ok = not contains_text(negative_out, marker_line)
    remove_file(fixture)
    if negative_ok then
      return ["verified", "positive probe's raw output contained the real matched line verbatim; negative probe's did not"]
    end
    return ["inconsistent", "positive_ok=true negative_ok=false -- find's real behavior did not match the simple substring-search contract this probe assumed"]
  end

  if looks_like_quoting_failure then
    print("[PLAN PHASE] Direct find.exe invocation failed with the known quoting-limitation signature -- attempting the ONE hand-written self-heal fallback (not a retry loop)...")
    let healed = verify_find_via_batch_fallback(fixture)
    remove_file(fixture)
    return healed
  end

  remove_file(fixture)
  return ["vacuous", "positive probe's output did not contain the matched line, and did not show the known quoting-failure signature either -- no self-heal attempted, this needs manual review"]
end

# The allowlist itself -- the ONLY names this tool will ever pass to
# verify_shell_command's dispatch below. Adding a new command here means
# writing a new hand-authored verify_* probe for it FIRST; there is
# deliberately no generic/automatic path that would execute an arbitrary
# discovered command with arbitrary arguments.
# =============================================================================
# ROOT-CAUSE FIX for a real, confirmed process failure: discover_shell_
# command_spec used to persist the LLM's freeform gherkin_text as the
# final .feature content whenever verify_shell_command's UNRELATED,
# hand-written probe happened to pass -- with nothing anywhere cross-
# checking that the LLM's specific claims (flags, described behaviour)
# had anything to do with what the probe actually verified. Found for
# real: findstr/find/which/where all shipped as "verified" while
# describing nonexistent flags or, for which/where, an entirely
# different, imaginary command (a GUI window manager). See
# spec_library/NEEDS_ATTENTION.md's "LLM-content vs probe mismatch"
# section for the full incident.
#
# Fix: for any command with a hand-written verify_* probe (i.e.
# is_shell_command_allowlisted), the PERSISTED .feature text must come
# from a hand-authored Gherkin block tied to that SAME probe -- added
# here, in this same table, at the point someone writes the probe --
# never from the LLM call, which stays useful only as a first-draft
# aid for genuinely new, not-yet-allowlisted commands (where
# verification_status honestly stays "not_allowlisted"/unverified).
make a function called hand_gherkin_for_command takes command_name returns gherkin
  if command_name == "findstr" then
    return "Feature: Windows findstr Command (native, literal substring search within a file)\n\n  Scenario: A line containing the literal search string is printed\n    Given a file containing the line \"PATLANG_MARKER_PRESENT here\" among other lines\n    When the user runs findstr \"PATLANG_MARKER_PRESENT\" on that file\n    Then the matching line is printed verbatim\n\n  Scenario: No match produces no matching line in the output\n    Given the same file, with a search string that appears nowhere in it\n    When the user runs findstr with that absent string on the same file\n    Then the output does not contain the absent marker text\n"
  end
  if command_name == "find" then
    return "Feature: Windows find.exe (System32) -- literal string search within text files\n\n  Scenario: A line containing the literal search string is printed\n    Given a file containing the line \"PATLANG_MARKER_PRESENT here\" among other lines\n    When the user runs find \"PATLANG_MARKER_PRESENT\" on that file\n    Then a \"---------- <FILENAME>\" header line is printed first, followed by the matching line verbatim (confirmed by direct fresh execution, not just this probe's own internal check)\n\n  Scenario: No match prints only the header, no matching line\n    Given the same file, with a search string that appears nowhere in it\n    When the user runs find on that file\n    Then only the \"---------- <FILENAME>\" header is printed; the output does not contain the absent marker text\n"
  end
  if command_name == "which" then
    return "Feature: which Command (Git/MSYS, resolves an executable's path via PATH)\n\n  Scenario: Resolve a command that exists on PATH\n    Given \"findstr\" is a real command available on PATH\n    When the user runs `which findstr`\n    Then it prints a POSIX-style path containing \"/findstr\" (no \".exe\" suffix -- this is Git's MSYS which, not native Windows)\n\n  Scenario: A nonexistent command name\n    Given a command name that does not exist anywhere on PATH\n    When the user runs `which <bogus-name>`\n    Then it prints \"no <bogus-name> in (...)\" -- this MSYS build's own specific not-found message, which echoes the searched-for name as part of the error text\n"
  end
  if command_name == "where" then
    return "Feature: Windows where Command (native, resolves an executable's path via PATH)\n\n  Scenario: Resolve a command that exists on PATH\n    Given \"findstr\" is a real command available on PATH\n    When the user runs `where findstr`\n    Then it prints a path ending in \"findstr.exe\"\n\n  Scenario: A nonexistent command name\n    Given a command name that does not exist anywhere on PATH\n    When the user runs `where <bogus-name>`\n    Then it prints an error, and the output contains no \".exe\" path\n"
  end
  if command_name == "curl" then
    return "Feature: curl (real HTTPS fetching -- closes the TLS gap PatLang's own networking has)\n\n  Scenario: Fetching a real HTTPS page returns its actual content\n    Given a real, live page (https://parslow.net/teaching/learning/se/bdd-retrofitting.html)\n    When `curl -s <url>` is run\n    Then the real page's actual HTML is returned verbatim (confirmed by checking for its real DOCTYPE and known content), NOT PatLang's own tcp_connect/tcp_read (no TLS support -- would only ever see a 301 redirect to this same HTTPS url, never real content)\n\n  Scenario: The real HTTP status code is reported\n    Given the same real page and a deliberately nonexistent one\n    When `curl -s -o NUL -w \"%{http_code}\" <url>` is run on each\n    Then the real page reports 200 and the nonexistent one reports 404\n\n  Note: this spec's own probe is deliberately coupled to one specific\n  real external page (rather than a neutral/generic test target) --\n  a deliberate choice, since the whole point of adding this spec was to\n  prove real HTTPS content from THIS SITE is actually fetchable via\n  curl, closing a real, named gap ([[patlang-synthesis-registry-experiments]])\n  found in PatLang's own native networking.\n"
  end
  return ""
end

make a function called is_shell_command_allowlisted takes command_name returns yes
  if command_name == "findstr" then
    return true
  end
  if command_name == "find" then
    return true
  end
  if command_name == "which" then
    return true
  end
  if command_name == "where" then
    return true
  end
  if command_name == "diff" then
    return true
  end
  if command_name == "curl" then
    return true
  end
  return false
end

# Real HTTPS fetch, added specifically to close the TLS gap
# [[patlang-synthesis-registry-experiments]] found: PatLang's own
# networking (tcp_connect/tcp_read/tcp_write) has no TLS support at
# all, so it can only ever reach an HTTPS-only site's plain-HTTP
# redirect, never real page content. curl (a real command-line tool
# already on this machine's PATH, confirmed via `where curl`) has real
# TLS built in -- shelling out to it is a legitimate way to get actual
# HTTPS content without PatLang needing its own TLS implementation.
make a function called verify_curl returns result
  let real_page = "https://parslow.net/teaching/learning/se/bdd-retrofitting.html"
  let missing_page = "https://parslow.net/definitely_not_a_real_page_xyz.html"

  let positive_out = exec_capture("curl", "-s", real_page)
  let positive_ok = contains_text(positive_out, "<!DOCTYPE html>") and contains_text(positive_out, "bdd")

  let status_out = exec_capture("curl", "-s", "-o", "NUL", "-w", "%{http_code}", real_page)
  let status_ok = contains_text(status_out, "200")

  let missing_status_out = exec_capture("curl", "-s", "-o", "NUL", "-w", "%{http_code}", missing_page)
  let missing_status_ok = contains_text(missing_status_out, "404")

  if positive_ok and status_ok and missing_status_ok then
    return ["verified", "curl fetches real HTTPS content -- a real, live page's actual HTML was retrieved verbatim (confirmed by checking for its real DOCTYPE and known content, not just 'some output happened'); curl's own -w \"%{http_code}\" reports the real HTTP status: 200 for a page that exists, 404 for one that doesn't -- genuine TLS-backed fetching PatLang's own networking cannot do natively"]
  end
  return ["inconsistent", "positive_ok=" + ("" + positive_ok) + " status_ok=" + ("" + status_ok) + " missing_status_ok=" + ("" + missing_status_ok)]
end

make a function called verify_shell_command takes command_name returns result
  if command_name == "findstr" then
    return verify_findstr()
  end
  if command_name == "find" then
    return verify_find()
  end
  if command_name == "which" then
    return verify_which()
  end
  if command_name == "where" then
    return verify_where()
  end
  if command_name == "diff" then
    return verify_diff()
  end
  if command_name == "curl" then
    return verify_curl()
  end
  return ["not_allowlisted", "no hand-written verification probe exists for '" + command_name + "' -- refusing to execute an unreviewed command"]
end

# --- Persisting a spec + its provenance/verification sidecar ---

make a function called json_escape takes s returns escaped
  let sb = sb_new()
  let i = 0
  while i < s.length do
    let c = char_code(s, i)
    if c == 34 then
      sb_push(sb, "\\\"")
    else
      if c == 92 then
        sb_push(sb, "\\\\")
      else
        if c == 10 then
          sb_push(sb, "\\n")
        else
          sb_push(sb, substr(s, i, 1))
        end
      end
    end
    let i = i + 1
  end
  return sb_str(sb)
end

make a function called write_shell_spec takes command_name, gherkin_text, source, model_name, fenced, verify_status, verify_note returns done
  let dir = shell_spec_dir()
  let feature_file = dir + "/" + command_name + ".feature"
  write_file(feature_file, gherkin_text + "\n")

  let sb = sb_new()
  sb_push(sb, "{\n")
  sb_push(sb, "  \"command\": \"" + json_escape(command_name) + "\",\n")
  sb_push(sb, "  \"category\": \"shell\",\n")
  sb_push(sb, "  \"feature_file\": \"" + command_name + ".feature\",\n")
  sb_push(sb, "  \"source\": \"" + json_escape(source) + "\",\n")
  sb_push(sb, "  \"model\": \"" + json_escape(model_name) + "\",\n")
  sb_push(sb, "  \"gherkin_fence_found\": " + ("" + fenced) + ",\n")
  sb_push(sb, "  \"gherkin_looks_valid\": " + ("" + looks_like_gherkin(gherkin_text)) + ",\n")
  sb_push(sb, "  \"verification_status\": \"" + json_escape(verify_status) + "\",\n")
  sb_push(sb, "  \"verification_note\": \"" + json_escape(verify_note) + "\"\n")
  sb_push(sb, "}\n")
  write_file(dir + "/" + command_name + ".meta.json", sb_str(sb))
  return true
end

# Resolves a bare command name to a real file path via `where` (native
# Windows, verified above) -- needed because exec_capture itself relies
# on the OS's own PATH search internally and never surfaces the
# resolved path back to the caller, but the GUI-detection gate below
# needs a real file to read PE header bytes from. Returns "" if `where`
# can't resolve it at all (treated as "can't confirm it's safe", not
# "assume it's fine").
make a function called resolve_command_path takes command_name returns path
  let raw = exec_capture("where", command_name)
  if contains_text(raw, "Could not find") then
    return ""
  end
  let lines = split_lines(raw)
  if to_num(list_len(lines)) == 0 then
    return ""
  end
  return full_trim_spec_discovery(lines[0])
end

make a function called full_trim_spec_discovery takes s returns trimmed
  let a = 0
  while (a < s.length) and ((char_code(s, a) == 32) or (char_code(s, a) == 9) or (char_code(s, a) == 13) or (char_code(s, a) == 10)) do
    let a = a + 1
  end
  let z = s.length
  while (z > a) and ((char_code(s, z - 1) == 32) or (char_code(s, z - 1) == 9) or (char_code(s, z - 1) == 13) or (char_code(s, z - 1) == 10)) do
    let z = z - 1
  end
  return substr(s, a, z - a)
end

# --- Orchestrator ---

make a function called discover_shell_command_spec takes command_name, model_name, hermes_model_name returns status_and_source
  print("[SPEC DISCOVERY] Processing shell command '" + command_name + "'...")

  let gherkin_result = llm_generate_gherkin_for_command(command_name, model_name)
  let gherkin_text = gherkin_result[0]
  let fenced = gherkin_result[1]
  let source = "ollama"
  let used_model = model_name

  # Escalate to hermes AT MOST ONCE per command -- not a retry loop --
  # only when the primary reply doesn't actually contain real Gherkin
  # content (checked via looks_like_gherkin, NOT via whether the
  # backtick fence happened to survive -- see that function's own
  # comment for why: fence presence is inconsistent across otherwise-
  # identical calls, content quality generally isn't). The length check
  # stays as a cheap pre-filter -- genuine fluff/refusal replies are
  # usually short, and it's a free check before the substring scan.
  if (gherkin_text.length < 60) or (not looks_like_gherkin(gherkin_text)) then
    print("[SPEC DISCOVERY] Primary (ollama) reply for '" + command_name + "' doesn't look like real Gherkin (length=" + ("" + gherkin_text.length) + ", looks_like_gherkin=" + ("" + looks_like_gherkin(gherkin_text)) + ") -- escalating to hermes once.")
    let hermes_result = llm_generate_gherkin_via_hermes(command_name, hermes_model_name)
    let hermes_text = hermes_result[0]
    let hermes_fenced = hermes_result[1]
    if (hermes_text.length >= 60) and looks_like_gherkin(hermes_text) then
      let gherkin_text = hermes_text
      let fenced = hermes_fenced
      let source = "hermes"
      let used_model = hermes_model_name
      print("[SPEC DISCOVERY] hermes fallback produced a usable spec for '" + command_name + "'.")
    else
      print("[SPEC DISCOVERY] hermes fallback ALSO didn't look like real Gherkin -- keeping the ollama reply as-is, flagged in metadata; no further escalation attempted.")
    end
  end

  if not looks_like_gherkin(gherkin_text) then
    print("[SPEC DISCOVERY] WARNING: '" + command_name + "'s final spec text doesn't look like real Gherkin (no Feature:/Scenario found) -- using it anyway, flagged in metadata.")
  end

  # Hard gate, checked BEFORE the allowlist even matters: a GUI
  # application launched via exec_capture never exits on its own (it
  # waits for user interaction), so probing one would hang the whole
  # discovery pipeline indefinitely -- this has to be ruled out before
  # anything else, not discovered by timing out. Resolves the command
  # to a real file path via `where` (a verified, allowlisted command in
  # its own right) and reads the PE header's own Subsystem field
  # directly, WITHOUT ever running the candidate executable.
  let verify_status = "not_allowlisted"
  let verify_note = "no hand-written verification probe exists for '" + command_name + "' -- refusing to execute an unreviewed command"
  let resolved_path = resolve_command_path(command_name)
  let pe_class = "not_pe"
  if resolved_path != "" then
    let pe_class = pe_subsystem_classify(resolved_path)
  end

  if (pe_class == "gui") or (pe_class == "other") then
    let verify_status = "gui_skipped"
    let verify_note = "'" + command_name + "' resolved to '" + resolved_path + "', whose PE header Subsystem field marks it as " + pe_class + ", not a console application -- never executed, since it would hang waiting for user interaction rather than exit on its own"
    print("[SPEC DISCOVERY] '" + command_name + "' is a GUI application (PE subsystem: " + pe_class + ") -- skipping execution entirely.")
  else
    if is_shell_command_allowlisted(command_name) then
      let probe = verify_shell_command(command_name)
      let verify_status = probe[0]
      let verify_note = probe[1]
      print("[SPEC DISCOVERY] Verification for '" + command_name + "': " + verify_status + " -- " + verify_note)
    else
      print("[SPEC DISCOVERY] '" + command_name + "' is not on the safety allowlist -- spec written as UNVERIFIED (not executed).")
    end
  end

  # See hand_gherkin_for_command's own header for why this exists: a
  # verified probe result must never be used to stamp "verified" onto
  # the LLM's independently-imagined content -- if a hand-authored
  # Gherkin block exists for this command (added alongside its verify_*
  # probe), that's what gets persisted, not gherkin_text.
  let hand_gherkin = hand_gherkin_for_command(command_name)
  if (verify_status == "verified") and (hand_gherkin != "") then
    let gherkin_text = hand_gherkin
    let source = "hand-authored"
    let used_model = ""
    let fenced = true
  else
    if (verify_status == "verified") and (hand_gherkin == "") then
      # A probe passed but nobody has yet written the matching
      # hand-authored Gherkin for it -- exactly the gap that let the
      # original bug happen. Flag it loudly rather than silently
      # trusting the LLM's unrelated text.
      let verify_note = verify_note + " -- WARNING: no hand_gherkin_for_command entry exists for '" + command_name + "' yet; the persisted .feature below is the LLM's own text, NOT cross-checked against this probe's actual behaviour -- add a hand_gherkin_for_command entry before trusting this spec as verified."
      print("[SPEC DISCOVERY] WARNING: '" + command_name + "' verified by probe but has no hand_gherkin_for_command entry -- persisting LLM content unchecked, flagged in metadata.")
    end
  end

  write_shell_spec(command_name, gherkin_text, source, used_model, fenced, verify_status, verify_note)
  print("[SPEC DISCOVERY] Wrote " + shell_spec_dir() + "/" + command_name + ".feature (+ .meta.json)")
  return [verify_status, source]
end
